## bulk-density

bulk-density-ask = How do you find the density of a granular substance?
bulk-density-title = The density of a granular substance
bulk-density-choice-bulk = Pour it into a measuring cylinder to a mark, weigh it and divide the mass by the volume — that is the substance’s density
bulk-density-choice-gaps = The mass over the volume poured is only the bulk density: there is air between the grains. The grains’ own volume comes from a liquid that fills the gaps and does not dissolve them
bulk-density-choice-water = Pour the substance into a cylinder of water and see how far the level rose — that way for any substance
bulk-density-substance-label = Substance
bulk-density-substance-salt = Table salt
bulk-density-substance-sugar = Sugar
bulk-density-substance-soda = Baking soda
bulk-density-substance-chalk = Chalk (powder)
bulk-density-substance-vitriol = Copper sulfate
bulk-density-substance-iron = Iron filings
bulk-density-setup = 100 mL measuring cylinders, a lab balance, a granular substance; water and sunflower oil
bulk-density-question = We pour the substance ({ $substance }) into a measuring cylinder to the 100 mL mark and weigh it. Then we pour in a liquid from another cylinder — up to the grains’ top. How do you find the density of a granular substance?
bulk-density-optional-shake = What if we shake it down?
